   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-statistics",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.10",
    "topic_title": "The Investigative Question Revisited and Data Collection",
    "stem": "In 2026, researchers recruit 800 adults and ask each one how many hours per week they currently exercise. The researchers do not tell anyone how much to exercise. They then measure each participant's blood pressure once a year for the next five years and compare blood pressure trends for frequent and infrequent exercisers. How should this study be classified?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "An experiment, because participants were measured repeatedly over a five-year period",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap treats tracking subjects over time as the defining feature of an experiment. Repeated measurement does not make a study experimental; an experiment requires the researcher to impose a treatment, and here exercise levels were chosen by the participants themselves. Fix: Ask whether the researcher assigned or imposed a treatment before considering how long or how often subjects were measured."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "A retrospective observational study, because exercise habits reflect participants' past behavior",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap confuses recording an existing condition with looking back at past records. Retrospective studies draw data from records or events that already happened; here the blood pressure data are gathered going forward over five years. Fix: Check whether the response data already exist when the study begins (retrospective) or will be collected from now on (prospective)."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "An experiment, because participants were sorted into frequent and infrequent exercise groups and compared",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This trap assumes that comparing groups means treatments were assigned. The groups here were formed from an existing characteristic the participants chose, not from a treatment the researchers imposed, so the study is observational. Fix: Ask whether the researchers decided which group each subject was in, or whether subjects arrived already belonging to a group."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "A prospective observational study",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "No treatment was imposed: the researchers only recorded exercise levels the participants already had, so the study is observational. Because the blood pressure data are collected going forward from the start of the study over the next five years, rather than taken from existing past records, it is prospective."
     }
    ]
   },
